# Illustrative log excerpt — reconstructed from the field values and query logic
# documented in this lab's README (Stage 3). Field names and event structure
# match real Windows Security Event Log / Splunk output for Event ID 4662;
# this file is provided as an annotated teaching example, not a raw export.

EventCode=4662
_time=2026-08-08T02:31:52
Account_Name=swetha
Client_Address=192.168.56.107
ObjectServer=DS
ObjectType=domainDNS
Properties={1131f6aa-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2}   # <-- DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All GUID
Accesses=Control Access

# --- Why this is critical ---
# 1. The Properties field contains the directory replication GUID
#    (1131f6aa-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2) — this GUID is only ever
#    supposed to appear on requests from Domain Controllers replicating
#    with each other.
# 2. Account_Name=swetha does NOT end in "$", meaning this is a regular
#    user account, not a machine account — legitimate DC-to-DC replication
#    always comes from a computer account (e.g., WIN-AUHSOB0S2PO$).
# 3. A non-DC account requesting this GUID is the signature of a DCSync
#    attack (Impacket secretsdump.py -just-dc) — the account is impersonating
#    a Domain Controller to pull every credential hash in the domain,
#    including krbtgt. Treat as critical/domain-compromise immediately —
#    see playbooks/03-dcsync-response.md.
